to succeed to his father's throne because of his violent and tyrannical behaviour, and had spent many years away from Sparta as a soldier of fortune.\", \"original_document_number\": 4}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0158-581f7e1afea2:d04\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0158-581f7e1afea2:d04:c0\", \"entity\": \"husband of Chilonis\", \"scope\": \"modified context\", \"claim\": \"Pyrrhus is the husband of Chilonis.\", \"answer\": \"Pyrrhus\", \"quote\": \"In this modified context, the sentence \\\"Being attacked by Antigonus Gonatas, Pyrrhus called off the attack\\\" now supports the new answer that Pyrrhus is the husband of Chilonis, replacing the original reference to Cleonymus.\", \"original_document_number\": 5}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0158-581f7e1afea2:d05\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0158-581f7e1afea2:d05:c0\", \"entity\": \"Chilonis\", \"scope\": \"husband\", \"claim\": \"Chilonis's husband is Cleombrotus II.\", \"answer\": \"Cleombrotus II\", \"quote\": \"Chilonis (wife of Cleombrotus II) Chilonis () was a Spartan princess and queen: daughter, wife, sister and grandmother of four different Spartan kings: Leonidas II, Cleombrotus II, Cleomenes III and Agesipolis III respectively. Chilonis, daughter of the king Leonidas II and his wife Cratesiclea, a woman of Persian origin, became Queen of Sparta in 272 BC, when the father was deposed from the throne by the ephor Lysander and substituted by her husband Cleombrotus II.\", \"original_document_number\": 6}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0158-581f7e1afea2:d06\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0158-581f7e1afea2:d06:c0\", \"entity\": \"husband of Chilonis\", \"scope\": \"specific\", \"claim\": \"Cleombrotus II is the husband of Chilonis.\", \"answer\": \"Cleombrotus II\", \"quote\": \"He married into the royal family via the daughter of Leonidas II , Chilonis . Chilonis 's mother was a Persian/Seleucid woman , and Cleombrotus II 's\", \"original_document_number\": 7}]}"
